
UNIT I – Foundations
Experiment 1 – Basic RL Environment
Procedure:
Import the required Python libraries.
Create an RL environment with states and actions.
Initialize the agent and starting state.
Select an action and execute it.
Observe the next state and reward.
Repeat the process and display the results.
Experiment 2 – Markov Decision Process
Procedure:
Define the states and possible actions.
Define the transition and reward information.
Initialize the agent in a starting state.
Select an action according to a policy.
Move to the next state and receive the reward.
Repeat the state-action process and analyze the policy.
Experiment 3 – Bellman Equation
Procedure:
Initialize the value of each state.
Define the rewards and discount factor.
Apply the Bellman equation.
Update the state values iteratively.
Continue until the values converge.
Display the final value function.
Experiment 4 – Multi-Armed Bandit
Procedure:
Create multiple actions/arms with different rewards.
Initialize the action-value estimates.
Select an action using ε-Greedy, UCB, or Softmax.
Receive the reward.
Update the estimated action value.
Repeat and compare exploration and exploitation.
UNIT II – Tabular Methods and Q-Networks
Experiment 5 – Policy Iteration and Value Iteration
Procedure:
Define the states, actions, rewards, and transitions.
Initialize the value function and policy.
Perform policy evaluation or value updates.
Improve the policy using the calculated values.
Repeat until the policy becomes stable.
Display the optimal policy.
Experiment 6 – Monte Carlo
Procedure:
Initialize the policy and value function.
Generate a complete episode.
Calculate the return from the collected rewards.
Update the value of visited states.
Repeat for multiple episodes.
Display the learned value function/policy.
Experiment 7 – TD(0), SARSA and Q-Learning
Procedure:
Initialize the Q-table.
Start the agent from an initial state.
Select an action using an exploration strategy.
Execute the action and observe reward and next state.
Update the Q-value using TD, SARSA, or Q-Learning.
Repeat until learning is completed.
Display the learned Q-table.
Experiment 8 – DQN, Double DQN and Dueling DQN
Procedure:
Create the RL environment.
Build a neural network to estimate Q-values.
Collect experiences from the environment.
Store experiences in replay memory.
Train the network using sampled experiences.
Use a target network for stable learning.
Evaluate and compare DQN, Double DQN, and Dueling DQN.
UNIT III – Policy Optimization
Experiment 9 – REINFORCE
Procedure:
Define the environment and policy network.
Initialize the policy parameters.
Generate an episode using the current policy.
Calculate the total return.
Compute the policy gradient.
Update the policy parameters.
Repeat until the policy improves.
Experiment 10 – A2C
Procedure:
Create the Actor and Critic networks.
Initialize their parameters.
Select actions using the Actor.
Calculate the state value using the Critic.
Calculate the advantage.
Update both Actor and Critic.
Repeat training and evaluate performance.
Experiment 11 – A3C
Procedure:
Create multiple worker agents.
Give each worker a copy of the environment.
Each worker interacts with the environment.
Calculate gradients from collected experiences.
Update the shared network asynchronously.
Continue until convergence.
Compare the learning performance.
Experiment 12 – PPO
Procedure:
Initialize the policy and value networks.
Collect experiences using the current policy.
Calculate rewards and advantages.
Calculate the probability ratio between old and new policies.
Apply PPO clipping.
Update the policy and value networks.
Evaluate the trained policy.
UNIT IV – Model-Based Reinforcement Learning
Experiment 13 – Model-Based RL
Procedure:
Initialize the environment.
Collect state, action, reward, and next-state data.
Learn the environment dynamics.
Use the learned model to predict future states.
Plan actions using the predictions.
Execute the selected action.
Evaluate the agent's performance.
Experiment 14 – Sampling-Based Planning
Procedure:
Define the initial state and goal.
Generate possible action sequences.
Simulate the sequences using the environment model.
Calculate the expected reward of each sequence.
Select the sequence with the highest reward.
Execute the best action.
Repeat until the goal is reached.
Experiment 15 – Model-Based Policy Optimization
Procedure:
Initialize the environment model and policy.
Collect real environment experiences.
Train the model using collected data.
Generate simulated experiences.
Optimize the policy using simulated data.
Test the policy in the real environment.
Compare sample efficiency with model-free RL.
Experiment 16 – Value-Equivalence Prediction
Procedure:
Define the environment and value function.
Collect state-action experiences.
Predict future values using the learned model.
Compare predicted and actual values.
Calculate prediction error.
Update the model/value function.
Repeat until prediction accuracy improves.
